in-the-life-of-a-loved-one-who-dies-is-part-of-grief">David Kessler</a>â€™s work. According to Harvard Business Review, Kessler is â€œthe worldâ€™s foremost expert on griefâ€. Heâ€™d worked closely with Elisabeth KÃ¼bler-Â­Ross, who was the reigning expert on grief before her own death in 2004. A large part of KÃ¼bler-Â­Rossâ€™s legacy is her classification of what she calls the five stages of grief: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance, as described in her 1969 book On Death and Dying. After you experience a death, she wrote, these stages can occur in any order, with one or more possibly not occurring at all. Just weeks after Renzoâ€™s death, Iâ€™d tricked myself into thinking I had jumped all the way to acceptance when really I was trying to outrun anger and depression. Bargaining hasnâ€™t been a big one for me, nor has denial. The facts have been pretty hard to ignore.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/ng-interactive/2026/jul/23/matthew-schnipper-rise-above-grief">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;APNItBLDbLwkTsYTcAwdR-eczBR32OFebjNNYJJvszwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;PO1pTY5027s_AzLjl4iy_TqYJM8RdAyUmSy4yBAYtG4ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JgZv2jIMkzaDGEn-PrLI_SJvS_SrYi78BGXCvRBEYBIƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;SwaN76EJBeQlNnj0iAIUPCZrvLi-eWIulqC1B6ZBmLgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bSpyNlh2Jw0ud_g8-GKbQGPuT7Fp10NCXWGnd-KoXDgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;39zlpZlDqnb29aI4Ui5H5-ECLms_ptSzS07Wgk4ugCwƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsum