rk`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;q7Ail1RuusdLgkXffPpiRDY-xy7pCyZ9r8IJ1hTlcfgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CW51U3BBFMOs9Jo-_TyWmt3G2ugfVcvb1hvoN_8Z8iUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqMatthew Schnipperƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8KiepJVKj5t3R_fRK6da7fSwMTSTaknMRcIPCUBm0ekƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx_https://www.theguardian.com/books/ng-interactive/2026/jul/23/matthew-schnipper-rise-above-griefƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;M5rvgLGk21BNcrZeRikz76sSqXu5Qf7O0fesc6XoqJAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxThu, 23 Jul 2026 11:00:43 GMTƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;UNg4yHuZtOUbpVs38f2-fezWI6ahBXwKpQoMFUjbjcEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkcrssƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2lkKRFrDkPo9RHPgdWSaXO6dLz0paY4VXJK8kYh0oYcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyì<p>After my 22-month-old child died, music and poetry brought catharsis and comfort</p><p>The day Renzo had his aneurysm, in the afternoon before it happened, I distinctly remember telling him that it was going to be a pleasure to watch him grow up. It seems ridiculous to write that here, the foreshadowing so on the nose as to be unbelievable. But itâ€™s true. To him and my wife Allegra, I said, â€œI love our little family.â€ After the struggles around his birth, job loss, the pandemic, it felt, finally, like we had made it to the other side. We had, I suppose, just not how I envisioned it.</p><p>My friends Jayson and Stacy recommended I get to know <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2021/jan/17/finding-meaning-in-the-life-of-a-loved-one-who-dies-is-part-of-grief">David Kessler</a>â€™s work. According to Harvard Business Review, Kessler is â€œthe worldâ€™s foremost expert on griefâ€. Heâ€™d worked closely with Elisabeth KÃ¼bler-Â­Ross, who was the reigning expert on grief before her own death in 2004. A large part of KÃ¼bler-Â­Rossâ€™s legacy is her classification of what she calls the five stages of grief: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance, as described in her 1969 book On Death and Dying. After you experience a death, she wrote, these stages can occur in any order, with one or more possibly not occurring at all. Just weeks after Renzoâ€™s death, Iâ€™d tricked myself into thinking I had jumped all the way to acceptance when really I was trying to outrun anger and depression. Bargaining hasnâ€™t been a big one for me, nor has denial. The facts have been pretty hard to ignore.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/ng-interactive/2026/jul/23/matthew-schnipper-rise-above-grief">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;URIBWUhbpx3DkFG6yk1KtAygxL6CDkm0M_cTJISZpnoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxMA grief therapist told me I should live to honor my son. How would I do that?ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;O8hJt9m06exg1bihAkO_BduKP3YPlCYFXzxyE67X-Jwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9CEJz7l8MxyG4aCOjXVm58jIj2QyY3onbGqw8yoUsoUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx_https://www.theguardian.com/books/ng-interactive/2026/jul/23/matthew-schnipper-rise-above-griefƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;4pTfP5l8q5Zuk8jCBrbDoMLLJ8QEGBfdZxt16Y-wtB0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerig